Date: Tue, 18 May 93 23:20:45 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: MATH: Friendly, Happy Chaitin

(I thought the title of this thread would help keep us all cheerful... :-)

Speaking of Chaitin, here's a speculation I had.

Chaitin is the founder of algorithmic information theory.  In this theory,
the amount of information in a string can be measured by the length of
the shortest possible program which can produce this string as output.
This is an attractive definition because it fits our intuition; a
string like 10101010... repeated 10^9 times is not very random, and indeed
a program to print out such a string would be fairly small.

(Under conventional information theory, all strings of a given length
might be said to have the same amount of information if they were all
equally likely.)

Another thing nice about Chaitin's definition is the information in the
concatenation of two strings is no more than the sum of the information
in the separate strings.  (It might be quite a bit less; for example if
the two strings were the same, then it would be hardly longer than the
one string since you'd just add a "repeat twice" to the program.)  At
worse you can concatenate the two programs for the two strings and get
out the concatenation of the strings.  (Chaitin chooses a particular
format for his programs so that this will always work.)

But Chaitin's definition has one big problem: it is not measurable.  There
is no way to tell, for a sufficiently large string, what the shortest
program is that will output it.  You'd think that you could just try
all possible short programs - after all, there are only a finite number -
but that doesn't work.  The reason is that some programs never stop.
You would have to wait forever to find out whether there is some short
program which doesn't seem to be doing very much but which maybe, just
maybe, might eventually bestir itself and output the very string you are
looking for.

One might think that a more careful analysis could eliminate these
long-running programs, but the unsolvability of the Halting Problem shows
that this is not the case.  This famous proof is related to Godel's
uncertainty theorem and establishes that there is no way in general to
tell, for any program, whether it will eventually halt.  More generally,
this also establishes that you can't tell whether a program might eventually
output some particular string or whether it's just going on forever.

This small blemish on Chaitin's results does not seem to bother most people
very much, but in my opinion it makes the whole concept virtually worthless.
One of the first things we learn in the philosophy of science is to deal
with measurable quantities.  A definition of information which has all
kinds of nice properties but can't be measured isn't of any use!

Given that the problem is these slow-running programs, my instinct is
to try to revise his definition by putting some time constraints as well
as space constraints on the allowable short programs.  Only those programs
which run in a time which is some specified function of the program length
("program" here would include the program plus any input) would count.
This way if some program doesn't produce the string in the allowed time,
it is rejected.  Now you can try all candidate short programs in a finite
amount of time, and get an unambiguous answer to the information content
of the string.

I'm sure this is a common thought, but clearly there are problems with it.
One problem is the arbitrariness of this unspecified function of the
program input length which produces our time limit.  There is a super-
ficially analogous arbitrariness in Chaitin's definition in terms of what
language the short programs are written in and what kind of machine they
run on.  But Chaitin shows that this leads to an uncertainty in the
information content which is small for large strings.

I wonder if such a result could be shown for various time-limit functions.
The obvious approach would be for time limits which were various polynomial
functions of the input length: n, n^2, n^3, n^4, etc.  Considering the
task of finding the shortest program which can output a string S under these
various limits.  With the limit "n" most programs might not finish and S's
information content would be close to S's length in bits.  With "n^2" a
lot of programs would finish and perhaps we find that S's information content
is smaller.  With "n^3" perhaps a few more programs manage to output S that
are even smaller.  As we go on to higher powers, though, we would expect
to hit diminishing returns.  For most strings, once we've gone up a bit
we've likely found the shortest program we're ever going to find.

We can't be SURE that eventually we wouldn't hit an even smaller program
(this would require solving the halting problem).  But as long as we
specify the time limit used we can at least get an unambiguous value.
But, different computers and languages might have different speeds.
As we go from, say, n^4 to n^5 on one computer we might see a sudden
drop in information content for S.  But on another computer which had
different timing we might have to go to n^6 to see that.  So as long as
we are staying at n^5 we would have two computers with very different
measures of S.  So we've lost uniqueness again.

This may not be that much of a problem if we consider that program
running times on different machines should not be inherently different
(at worst, we simulate one computer using the other one).  Chaitin
discusses this in the context of program size and I don't remember the
details.  But if the running times are similar on all machines then I
think we could still get a unique measure.

There may be some properties of Chaitin algorithmic complexity that the
time-limited versions lack.  If these can be overcome, though, we'd
have a more useful tool for analyzing information and its role in nature.

Hal Finney

