(or divining my future behavior) has no place on this list.

On to Mr. Raymond's next point, despite his rudeness:

>
>So tell me, Tim.  Does the set of WFFs indexed by Chaitin's omega exhaust all
>WFFs?  Do you understand the proof well enough to commit yourself to an answer?
>-- 
>                                        Eric S. Raymond <esr@snark.thyrsus.com>

Any statement in the predicate calculus (which includes propositional
logic, and its grammatically correct or well-formed formulas (WFFs)) can be
Godelized, that is, each possible statement can be uniquely coded or
labeled with its Godel number (via a complicated process). Chaitin's scheme
does the same thing, so in this sense I would say that, yes, all statements
of the propositional calculus, and hence all WFFs, can be "Chaitinized."

Chaitin shows how expressions may be arithmetized, and his code for doing
so is available from him. The details are (expectedly) very messy,  just as
Godel's numbering scheme was similarly messy (and occupied most of his
paper).

Chaitin's work, like Turing's, is phrased more in terms of recursively
enumerable functions (or S-expressions in the lambda calculus) than in
terms of well-formed formulas of propositional logic. I'm neither a
logician nor a set theoretician, but nothing I have seen indicates that the
Turing/Godel/Post/Church/Kleene model of computation (usually called the
Universal Turing Machine, UTM) is not adequate. Certain speculations,
notably by David Deutsch and Roger Penrose, that quantum-mechanical
phenonomena may be needed to transcend the Turing/Post/etc. mode, remain
speculations. 

Lest there be any confusion, Chaitin's work is a direct extension of the
earlier work and in my opinion warrants the extension of the name of the
"standard model" to the "Turing/Godel/Post/Church/Kleene/Chaitin" model.
Each of these formulators of the theory of computation put his own twist on
the theory, his own unique flavor. Chaitin's flavor was to talk about the
intrinsic information content of a statement and how a theory of some
information size could not then talk about bits beyond a certain
point...they would be indistinguishable from the results of random coin
tosses. An n-bit theory has limits.

Anything a UTM can accept as input, Chaitin's EVAL program can also accept.
 Any statement (a computer program, for example) that can be converted into
an S-expression (in the form of the pure LISP Chaitin has implemented) can
be fed directly into Chaitin's EVAL program. To emphasize: S-expressions
can handle all computable functions, that is, there is nothing computable
that LISP, being recursively-complete, cannot handle.

(The reason for Chaitin's choice of LISP was very similar to the reason
John Koza chose LISP for his recent work on genetic programming: LISP is
its own parse tree and "randomly constructed" LISP S-Expressions are
already "runnable" programs...most of them will of course halt the machine
fairly quickly (an aspect of Chaitin's work), but at least they'll be
accepted as legal inputs to the EVAL program. In a sense, S-expressions are
automatically WFFs for the machine. By the way, the connections between
Koza's universe of evolving S-expressions and Chaitin's S-expressions
warrant further study, though of course the specific choice of a language
is not the key issue--C or Pascal would've also worked, with more work in
each case. Still, some intriguing parallels, and and an evolving organism
may truly said to be "computing part of Omega," a point Chaitin makes
well.)

In conclusion, there is no WFF--as I understand Eric's use of the term in
this context--which cannot be converted into the form Chaitin's EVAL takes.
Chaitin's results apply to all theorems. (No, to head off another
debatefest, this does *not* mean all theorems are random!)

Cheers, I think.

--Tim May


