Subject: Happy little Chaitins, was Re: Chaitin

(Perhaps I should only respond to this post by Hal Finney *after*
responding to his "Happy little Chaitins" post (or whatever...actually, I
really liked that title, so I'll rename Hal's thread to convey the magickal
power of it).

Hal Finney writes:

>This would mean, would it not, that Omega is uncomputable.  In particular,
>in order to find what the probability is that a random program will halt,
>you need to be able to decide whether a given program will halt.  (Other-
>wise you are trying to determine the probability of black or white balls
>in a bag with your eyes closed.)  But you can't decide whether a given
>program will halt.  Hence you can't compute Omega.

Omega is uncomputable. Various terms might be computed by lots of
computation and by "experimentation" (e.g., bombarding a particular UTM
model with lots randomly-constructed S-expressions of increasing length
might produce:

         Omega for this machine ~=~ 0.983017.... (prob. that machine will
halt when a randomly selected program in run)


>The ease of finding the early bits of Omega is misleading.  Small programs
>don't feel the impact of the halting problem proof.  Only programs large
>enough to encode the ability to run other programs (basically, big enough
>to be a language interpreter) have undecidable halting characteristics.
>Once you are far enough out in Omega to be dealing with programs this big
>you are not going to be able to find any more bits, at least not by this
>means.

Agreed.
>
>I'm unclear on Chaitin's other proposals for finding Omega.  Given the
>the fact that Omega can't be axiomatized, I don't see how anyone can write
>down a method to discover its value.

The experimental method I mentioned is one way to get an approximation to
Omega, for some particular Omega (machines vary, languages for programs
vary, axiom sets vary, so there won't be some single, mystical
"Omega"...any given configuration will have its own, which is exactly what
one would expect given that Omega encodes the answers to halting problem,
which will vary depending on a lot of factors).

Chaitin writes:

"We have seen that Omega is about as random, patternless, unpredictable and
incomprehensible as possible; the pattern of its bit sequence defies
understanding. However with computations in the limit, which is equivalent
to having an oracle for the halting problem, Omega seems quite
understandable: it becomes a computable sequence. Biological evolution is
the nearest thing to an infinite computation in the limit we will ever see:
it is a computation with molecular components that has proceeded for 10^9
years in parallel over the surface of the earth. That amount of computing
could easily produce a good approximation to Omega, except that that is not
the goal of biological evolution....

"Perhaps biological structures are simple and easy to understand only if
one has an oracle for the halting problem."


-Tim May
