Timothy C. May writes:
> I'd urge people on this list to be more careful about immediately
> dismissing what they don't understand, or haven't taken the time to think
> about. Intuition is a dangerous thing...to many, the whole idea of
> "reversible computations" is self-evidently wrong ("it doesn't smell
> right"), just as theories of quantum mechanics, continental drift,
> relativity, and even evolution struck some as intuitively wrong. I get the
> very same "ignorant skepticism" (and by this I mean absolutely no criticsm
> of Eric, Robin, Ray, or Mike....this just describes the phenomenon

  The criticism I made of the original article was that it was vague
in parts and seemed to be deliberately controversal ("arithmetic" is
"random") I'm still a little unclear on exactly what Chaitin has proven.
Omega is a binary string of digits -- each digit representing whether or not
a particular diophantine equation in a certain family has infinite or finite
number of solutions. He picked a certain diophantine equation family which is
equivalent to a UTM which also represents a formal system. By
showing that each digit in Omega is, for all intents and purposes,
random he demonstrates that whether or not a particular mathematical
statement is decidable is random, correct?

My only question is, does his particular diophantine family exhaust
all possible statements? Or is his particular encoding only able to
represent a small chunk of the vast theorem space.

My other problem is with the "for all intents and purposes" part.
Just because it's hard to compute what a particular digit of
Omega is (for k large, like Ramsey's number, or the number of bits
a Busy Beaver with >12 states can generate) doesn't mean that
it can't be calculated in principle. In principle, I can calculate
an arbitrary digit in pi's expansion, but past a certain
digit it starts to become infeasable. Does this mean the digits of
pi are ``random''? (a popular and famous question regularly asked in
sci.math) Is it fair to postulate the concept of infinity in
a mathematical paper but rule out infinite time for computation?

Make no mistake, I am not out to attack his result. Chaitin is certainly
much more knowledgable in math/cs than I am and is probably correct.
I'm just trying piece together the details. Most excerpts on this
list (including semi-popularizations), only scratch the surface
and leave you only wanting more.  (e.g. the dreaded 
"there is an equation proving so-and-so, but it is too complex to be
stated here, or beyond the scope of this paper or audience")

-Ray

