Date: Wed, 19 May 93 8:37:44 CDT
From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Subject: Chaitin, Randomness, and Explanations on this List

Ray:

> My only question is, does his particular diophantine family exhaust
> all possible statements? Or is his particular encoding only able to
> represent a small chunk of the vast theorem space.

I was mistaken about this before but have since seen the light.  As
Tim has mentioned, the truth of any theorem in any axiom system
can be coded as whether some particular turing machine halts.  Since
the particular diophantine equation (the one with 17,000 variables)
Chaitin uses is equivalent to a UTM, it can represent all of
theorem space.

Now, Omega is the probability that a random turing machine
will halt.  Turns out it's trivial to turn Omega into a solution
to the halting problem -- which by itself is sufficient to
show that Omega is uncomputable.

Sorry for the mistaken conclusion (given as a response to Eric
in a previous note).

derek

