Date: Wed, 19 May 93 14:53:54 +0100
From: Rich Walker <grlw1@cus.cam.ac.uk>
Subject: AI: Detecting it

(This is vaguely related to the last AI thread. Only vaguely)

While working on building robots, I noted the obvious hole in the standard
(Turing) test for intelligence, so I came up with my own. The other day, I
dumped it in comp.ai, during a thread on the Turing Test, and got an interesting
piece of feedback. Anyway, I redrafted it, and thought I'd pass it round here,
to see what anyone thought of it...

(apologies if I posted the previous version of it; I'm just trying to
disseminate a more _useful_ meme than Turing's for intelligence-definitions)

Lasagne Test, Version Two, with Universal Quantification:

I arrive home in the evening. My robot has put out
my slippers in front of my chair, marked a couple of particularly
interesting articles in the paper, video'd a program I wanted to watch & so
on. I sit; it pours my ice-cold beer, and passes the paper. I read a
little, drink a little, and then turn to it and say `What's for dinner'?

Robot has performed the following calculation:
   I didn't eat this morning.
   I had liquid lunch (probably)
   I like these things
   These things are in the kitchen
  So it can cook this list of things.
  I want something substantial.

=> `I took the liberty of preparing a lasagne, Sir'
  `How the f' did you know I wanted lasagne?'

The test generalises; the essence of it is:

  If I have some desire, and it is possible to, from my patterns of behaviour
  and from known and expected circumstances, predict this behaviour, then a
  machine which will, most of the time, predict this desire can usefully be
  declared intelligent.

I'd draft a more formal statement of this, but I _hate_ trying to do so in
ASCII:

(i member_of humans ^ x desire_of i ^ r : r a processing system,
  ( knows(r) (behavioural patterns(i) ^  world state(i)) => knows(r) x)
  => ( (Pr( predicts(r) x) > f(x)) => intelligent(r) )
  might do, for the moment, where
  f:(desires)-> [0,1] ; f(x) = (sum (h member_of humans) Pr( predicts(h) x)) )
                                -------------------------------------------
                                                 | humans |

See what I mean? Like the Turing Test, this has the useful property of being
applicable to any processing system, down to (:->) and including humans...

 There's still a problem with ironing out how much of the state of i needs to
be known by r, but still.

(I must admit, if I thought of it now, it'd be the Chilli test...)

